  "abstract": "We classify the rational points on one explicit genus-eleven curve defined by regular-quadruple and regular-quintuple relations in the rational Diophantine septuple problem. The curve is a biquadratic cover of a genus-two base. An exact bigonal correspondence identifies the two-dimensional Prym of its genus-four intermediate cover with a genus-two Jacobian up to isogeny. A complete 2-descent over a sextic field of discriminant -44496 proves that this Jacobian has rank one. Two known rational points in a single residue disk at five determine the annihilating differential modulo five through a tiny integral. Ten residue disks have a unit differential and two have a simple zero; the corresponding bound of fourteen intermediate rational points is attained. The remaining two disks are excluded by nonsquare normalized residues of the second covering character. Exact lifting leaves precisely twenty-four rational normalization points on the full cover, all with zero entries or poles. The proof requires neither a saturated Mordell-Weil basis nor global Coleman integration. We also separate this component from the occupied native branch of the motivating almost-septuple by an exact factorization and rational control value. Thus the result is a complete decision on the specified component, not an obstruction to that occupied branch or a resolution of the septuple problem. A portable Sage supplement verifies the arithmetic, correspondence, local count, branch separation and generic twenty-one-root decoder.",
